{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Kecebas2017-designII-12layers",
  "Source": "M. A. Kecebas, M. P. Menguc, A. Kosar, and K. Sendur, \"Passive radiative cooling design with broadband optical thin-film filters,\" J. Quant. Spectrosc. Radiat. Transf. (2017), article in press, pp. 1-8 (volume not printed in this copy). doi:10.1016/j.jqsrt.2017.03.046 - Fig. 5(b), p. 5",
  "Notes": "Design II variant with 12 absorption-segment layers (4 triplets, 200 nm each)\nSpecification: High solar reflectance (280 nm - 2.5 um), high emission 8-13 um using Al2O3/SiO2/TiO2 triplets.\nReported: Average emission in 8-13 um 78 % (Fig. 5(b) caption).\nNotes: 16 dielectric layers (12 in the absorption segment = 4 triplets of 200 nm, plus 4 x 20 nm solar segment), total 2480 nm. Triplet order from Fig. 3(d): Al2O3 on top, SiO2, TiO2 at the bottom, repeated; solar segment as in Design I (TiO2 on Ag, SiO2, TiO2, SiO2). Solar segment assumed unchanged (4 x 20 nm). The paper's stack is Si substrate / 50 nm Ag / dielectrics; Si has no index in the paper and is not an allowed reference, so the 50 nm Ag is treated as the (opaque) substrate. This is an approximation: Fig. 6(b) shows 50 nm Ag on Si reflecting ~97-98 % from ~0.5 to 13.5 um, so the Ag is effectively opaque in the IR, but a little light may reach the Si at short wavelengths. No optical constants are given for TiO2 (anatase), SiO2, Al2O3 or Ag (single-layer absorption is only plotted in Fig. 3(a)-(c)); database names are used, and their 8-13 um phonon absorption (the purpose of the absorption segment) may not be represented by generic dispersions, so the 8-13 um emission may not be reproducible. The paper lists the stack from the Ag upward (substrate first); it is reversed here so layer 1 is next to air. Paper averages are plain wavelength averages (Sec. 2, p. 3). Normal incidence unless stated; unpolarised assumed. Ag here is Yang et al. 2015 (refractiveindex.info, 0.27-24.9 um), which covers the infrared; the generic TiO2 and SiO2 have no infrared absorption, so the emission averages cannot be reproduced with this design - see the -MIR variants.\nLayers from Fig. 5(b), p. 5; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nNot checked: the paper reports this design only as plots.",
